{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '42,150p'; cat references.bib; cat references_provenance.json\"","aggregated_output":"    42\t\n    43\t\\paragraph{Norm, efficiency, and feature learning.}\n    44\t\\citet{Liu2022OmnigrokGB} relate grokking to training and test loss landscapes as a function of weight norm. \\citet{Varma2023ExplainingGT} explain delayed generalization through competition between memorizing and generalizing circuits with different norm efficiency and learning speed. \\citet{Kumar2023GrokkingAT} study a lazy-to-rich transition, including polynomial regression in which delayed generalization occurs without weight decay. These accounts motivate different diagnostics: initial norm, efficiency of task-relevant computations, and evolving features are not interchangeable quantities. Our norm-rescaling and zero-decay controls address limited observable consequences. They do not measure circuit efficiency, kernel alignment, or the onset of feature learning, and cannot select among these explanations.\n    45\t\n    46\t\\paragraph{Capacity and duration.}\n    47\t\\citet{Davies2023Unifying} propose a pattern-learning-speed framework connecting grokking and double descent, including changes in model capacity. \\citet{Nakkiran2020Double} show that model size and training duration can each produce nonmonotone test behavior. These works motivate joint attention to width and observation horizon, rather than an assumption that larger networks must generalize better at every endpoint. Our narrow width sweep is not an estimate of a double-descent curve. Finally, AdamW decouples weight decay from the adaptive loss-gradient update \\citep{Loshchilov2019AdamW}. Matching the nominal decay factor is therefore a useful optimizer description, not proof of equal effective regularization or equal post-fit exposure.\n    48\t\n    49\t\\section{Method}\n    50\t\\subsection{Problem Setup and Evaluation Target}\n    51\tThe task is classification of $y=(a+b)\\bmod p$ for integer residues $a,b\\in\\{0,\\ldots,p-1\\}$ with $p=97$. The full domain contains $p^2$ ordered input pairs. The supplied development and final resources partition that domain into disjoint pools of 4,704 and 4,705 inputs. Each seed defines a training/evaluation split within its pool, with training fraction 0.6. Development splits contain 2,822 training and 1,882 evaluation examples; final splits contain 2,823 and 1,882, respectively. The fixed pools and seed-specific splits, rather than the full operation table, define the measured generalization target. No additional task with recorded results is present.\n    52\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/resources/search/dataset.json#/pool_size]]\n    53\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/resources/final/dataset.json#/splits]]\n    54\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    55\t\n    56\tFor hidden width $w$, the logits are\n    57\t\\begin{equation}\n    58\t z_\\theta(a,b)=\\operatorname{GELU}\\!\\left(E^{(1)}_{a,:}+E^{(2)}_{b,:}+c\\right)W+d,\n    59\t \\label{eq:model}\n    60\t\\end{equation}\n    61\twhere $E^{(1)},E^{(2)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{p\\times w}$, $c\\in\\mathbb{R}^{w}$, $W\\in\\mathbb{R}^{w\\times p}$, and $d\\in\\mathbb{R}^{p}$. Full-batch cross-entropy is minimized using AdamW in float32 on CPU. Widths are $32,128,256$, with $292w+97$ trainable parameters: 9,441, 37,473, and 74,849. All biases start at zero. The model has no attention, recurrence, or depth intervention. The inspected final training source implements this architecture directly (Supplementary Information, Section~\\ref{sec:source}).\n    62\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L801]]\n    63\t% @evidence[[derived/observations.json#/rows]]\n    64\t\n    65\t\\subsection{Hypotheses and Interventions}\n    66\tWe distinguish two observable predictions. A \\emph{monotone width-benefit prediction} expects earlier fitting and no reduction in held-out endpoint accuracy as width increases at fixed training horizon. A \\emph{separated-timescale explanation} allows earlier fitting alongside worse short-horizon generalization; it predicts that fitting onset alone will not order held-out performance. A stronger claim that width systematically shortens the post-fit delay would require comparable, observed generalization onsets across widths. Our evidence can challenge the monotone prediction but cannot establish that stronger claim.\n    67\t\n    68\tInitialization is coupled by seed across widths: Gaussian embedding and readout tensors are drawn at the maximum width, then sliced. Embeddings are multiplied by a common multiplier $m$, and the readout is scaled by $m/\\sqrt{w}$. This pairing reduces arbitrary initialization differences without making different widths functionally equivalent. The studied condition uses learning rate $\\eta=0.003$, weight decay $\\lambda=1.0$, and $m=0.5$. It is common across widths and seeds, not adapted using training curves. It was selected using development evaluations; those observations therefore remain selection-conditioned rather than confirmatory.\n    69\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L803]]\n    70\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/controllers/studied/src/program.py#L37]]\n    71\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    72\t\n    73\tEach retained width--seed cell contains the following matched conditions:\n    74\t\\begin{itemize}\n    75\t\\item \\textbf{Studied:} the common configuration above.\n    76\t\\item \\textbf{Reference:} $\\eta=0.001$, $\\lambda=1.0$, $m=1.0$, with the same width, seed, split, and horizon. Because both learning rate and initialization differ, this is a configuration comparison, not a component-specific test.\n    77\t\\item \\textbf{No decay:} the studied configuration with $\\lambda=0$. This isolates removal of decay within the measured setting, not its necessity for all delayed generalization.\n    78\t\\item \\textbf{Norm matched:} the studied initialization is rescaled so its global Euclidean parameter norm equals that of the same-seed width-128 initialization. Learning rate and decay are unchanged. This matches one scalar, not capacity, layerwise geometry, or initial function.\n    79\t\\end{itemize}\n    80\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L795]]\n    81\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L821]]\n    82\tAt width 128 the norm-matching target is the original model itself. The retained initial/final state hashes and scientific metric trajectories match the studied condition there. This duplicate supplies neither an independent replication nor an additional delayed event.\n    83\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    84\t\n    85\t\\subsection{Training Protocol and Event Definitions}\n    86\tDevelopment observations use seeds 101 and 211 and a horizon of 6,000 updates. Final observations use seeds 60613, 70717, and 80821 with a planned horizon of 24,000 updates. Accuracy is recorded at initialization and every 100 updates. All four conditions in a retained cell use the same split. Distinct development/final pools prevent exact input overlap across those resources, but the longer final horizon also changes alongside the seeds and data. No paired duration-only contrast is available.\n    87\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    88\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    89\t\n    90\tFor a checkpoint accuracy sequence $A(t)$, define a sustained threshold onset as\n    91\t\\begin{equation}\n    92\t T_q[A]=\\min\\{t_j:\\ A(t_j),A(t_{j+1}),A(t_{j+2})\\geq q\\},\n    93\t \\qquad\n    94\t \\fit=T_{0.99}[A_{\\mathrm{train}}],\\quad\n    95\t \\gen=T_{0.95}[A_{\\mathrm{eval}}].\n    96\t \\label{eq:onset}\n    97\t\\end{equation}\n    98\tThe onset is the first checkpoint in the qualifying triplet, not the confirmation time. When both onsets exist, the delay is $\\max(0,\\gen-\\fit)$. A delayed event requires a delay of at least 1,000 updates. If either onset is absent, the evaluator records censoring and a normalized capped-delay value of one. This last convention is a reporting functional, not an observed event time. Three consecutive checkpoints suppress single-checkpoint spikes but do not guarantee persistent accuracy after the qualifying window. We retain the distinction between threshold-defined delayed generalization and a mechanistically verified transition.\n    99\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L1001]]\n   100\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   101\t\n   102\t\\subsection{Reporting, Missingness, and Uncertainty}\n   103\tThe unit of replication is the seed, not the evaluation example, checkpoint, or matched condition. We report all retained seed values and descriptive sample standard deviations where appropriate. Paired differences use only seeds shared by the compared widths. We do not replace the planned widest-minus-narrowest final contrast with an intermediate-minus-narrowest contrast without explicitly naming that change in estimand. Missing width records have no performance outcome; they are not measured losses, censored trajectories, or zero-accuracy models.\n   104\t\n   105\tFor transparency, we also give exact binomial intervals for delayed-event counts conditional on retained records. These intervals describe what a hypothetical independent-seed sampling model would allow; the small sample and unknown missingness preclude a population interpretation. No inferential test uses examples or checkpoints as extra replications. Failed final qualification applies independently of these descriptive calculations. The final summary supplies no criterion-specific diagnostics, so neither the observed missingness nor the retained controls can certify validity or identify the cause of non-qualification.\n   106\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/limitations]]\n   107\t% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#/cells]]\n   108\t\n   109\t\\section{Results}\n   110\t\\subsection{Evidence Coverage and Scientific Qualification}\n   111\tTable~\\ref{tab:coverage} gives the full final observation pattern for the studied condition. Only five of nine planned width--seed cells are retained: three at width 32, two at width 128, and none at width 256. All retained cells contain complete-horizon trajectories for the matched conditions. At width 32, failure to observe the sustained generalization threshold is right censoring at the recorded horizon. At width 256, absence of trajectories provides no information about onset or endpoint accuracy. The missing width-128 seed is likewise unavailable, not evidence against generalization.\n   112\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   113\t\n   114\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   115\t\\centering\n   116\t\\caption{Final coverage for the studied condition. C: recorded trajectory, generalization onset censored at 24,000 updates; D: recorded delayed event; M: missing record. A missing cell is not assigned an accuracy or a censoring time. All conditions in each retained cell were recorded.}\n   117\t\\label{tab:coverage}\n   118\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n   119\t\\toprule\n   120\tSeed & Width 32 & Width 128 & Width 256 \\\\\n   121\t\\midrule\n   122\t60613 & C & D & M \\\\\n   123\t70717 & C & D & M \\\\\n   124\t80821 & C & M & M \\\\\n   125\t\\bottomrule\n   126\t\\end{tabular}\n   127\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   128\t\\end{table}\n   129\t\n   130\tThe final evaluation completed but did not qualify. We preserve that conclusion rather than interpreting selection as scientific acceptance. No supplied diagnostic distinguishes a statistical/performance failure from a validity or adaptivity failure. Retained observations can describe the measured trajectories, but they cannot restore claims that would require qualification. In particular, neither complete trajectories in individual cells nor successful execution of local controls establishes general validity of the final comparison.\n   131\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.status]]\n   132\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.outcome]]\n   133\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.correct]]\n   134\t\n   135\t\\subsection{Width Accelerates Fitting but Does Not Order Short-Horizon Accuracy}\n   136\tThe short-horizon comparisons show a consistent fitting order. In the studied condition, width-32 fitting onsets are 2,200 and 2,800 updates; width 128 fits at 300 updates in both seeds; width 256 fits at 200 in both seeds. The paired widest-minus-narrowest differences are thus $-2,000$ and $-2,600$ updates. Yet held-out endpoint accuracy is highest at width 32, lowest at width 128, and intermediate at width 256 in each seed (Table~\\ref{tab:development}, Figure~\\ref{fig:endpoint}). Earlier training fit therefore does not order generalization at this horizon.\n   137\t% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#/cells]]\n   138\t% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#/paired/development_256_minus_32]]\n   139\t\n   140\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   141\t\\centering\n   142\t\\caption{Studied condition at the 6,000-update development horizon. Accuracy is mean $\\pm$ sample SD over the two seeds, in percent. Onsets are listed by seed rather than averaged with unobserved values. No sustained held-out onset is observed at any width.}\n   143\t\\label{tab:development}\n   144\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n   145\t\\toprule\n   146\tWidth & Parameters & Training-fit onsets & Held-out accuracy (\\%) \\\\\n   147\t\\midrule\n   148\t32 & 9,441 & 2,200; 2,800 & $74.68\\pm4.47$ \\\\\n   149\t128 & 37,473 & 300; 300 & $29.33\\pm3.68$ \\\\\n   150\t256 & 74,849 & 200; 200 & $39.35\\pm3.49$ \\\\\n@misc{Power2022GrokkingGB,\n  author = {Alethea Power and Yuri Burda and Harri Edwards and Igor Babuschkin and Vedant Misra},\n  title = {{Grokking: Generalization Beyond Overfitting on Small Algorithmic Datasets}},\n  year = {2022},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2201.02177},\n  eprint = {2201.02177},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv}\n}\n\n@misc{Liu2022OmnigrokGB,\n  author = {Ziming Liu and Eric J. Michaud and Max Tegmark},\n  title = {{Omnigrok: Grokking Beyond Algorithmic Data}},\n  year = {2023},\n  howpublished = {International Conference on Learning Representations},\n  eprint = {2210.01117},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv}\n}\n\n@misc{Kumar2023GrokkingAT,\n  author = {Tanishq Kumar and Blake Bordelon and Samuel J. Gershman and Cengiz Pehlevan},\n  title = {{Grokking as the Transition from Lazy to Rich Training Dynamics}},\n  year = {2024},\n  howpublished = {International Conference on Learning Representations},\n  eprint = {2310.06110},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv}\n}\n\n@misc{Varma2023ExplainingGT,\n  author = {Vikrant Varma and Rohin Shah and Zachary Kenton and J{\\'a}nos Kram{\\'a}r and Ramana Kumar},\n  title = {{Explaining Grokking Through Circuit Efficiency}},\n  year = {2023},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2309.02390},\n  eprint = {2309.02390},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv}\n}\n\n@misc{Nanda2023Progress,\n  author = {Neel Nanda and Lawrence Chan and Tom Lieberum and Jess Smith and Jacob Steinhardt},\n  title = {{Progress Measures for Grokking via Mechanistic Interpretability}},\n  year = {2023},\n  howpublished = {International Conference on Learning Representations},\n  eprint = {2301.05217},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv}\n}\n\n@misc{Davies2023Unifying,\n  author = {Xander Davies and Lauro Langosco and David Krueger},\n  title = {{Unifying Grokking and Double Descent}},\n  year = {2023},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2303.06173},\n  eprint = {2303.06173},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv}\n}\n\n@misc{Nakkiran2020Double,\n  author = {Preetum Nakkiran and Gal Kaplun and Yamini Bansal and Tristan Yang and Boaz Barak and Ilya Sutskever},\n  title = {{Deep Double Descent: Where Bigger Models and More Data Hurt}},\n  year = {2020},\n  howpublished = {International Conference on Learning Representations},\n  eprint = {1912.02292},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv}\n}\n\n@misc{Loshchilov2019AdamW,\n  author = {Ilya Loshchilov and Frank Hutter},\n  title = {{Decoupled Weight Decay Regularization}},\n  year = {2019},\n  howpublished = {International Conference on Learning Representations},\n  eprint = {1711.05101},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv}\n}\n{\n  \"count\": 8,\n  \"generated_at\": \"2026-11-13T04:37:33.692320+00:00\",\n  \"references\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ref.arxiv.2201.02177\",\n      \"citation_key\": \"Power2022GrokkingGB\",\n      \"title\": \"Grokking: Generalization Beyond Overfitting on Small Algorithmic Datasets\",\n      \"year\": 2022,\n      \"venue\": \"arXiv preprint arXiv:2201.02177\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2201.02177\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-13T04:14:51.024610+00:00\",\n      \"consulted_at\": \"2026-11-13T04:37:33.692320+00:00\",\n      \"consulted_pages\": [\n        1\n      ],\n      \"extracted_pages\": [\n        1,\n        2\n      ],\n      \"consultation_scope\": \"Abstract and opening first-page text; full-paper review is not claimed.\",\n      \"consulted_excerpt\": \"source_evidence/literature/2201.02177_opening.txt\",\n      \"original_pdf_sha256\": \"305dbd1256e1ac198dde517dda42b4f0a7eb9a9d3f4c03527c125b03eb9c62fc\",\n      \"scope\": \"exact user-authorized original; no citation/recommendation traversal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ref.arxiv.2210.01117\",\n      \"citation_key\": \"Liu2022OmnigrokGB\",\n      \"title\": \"Omnigrok: Grokking Beyond Algorithmic Data\",\n      \"year\": 2023,\n      \"venue\": \"International Conference on Learning Representations\",\n     