acted_pages\": [\n        1,\n        2\n      ],\n      \"consultation_scope\": \"Abstract and opening first-page text; full-paper review is not claimed.\",\n      \"consulted_excerpt\": \"source_evidence/literature/1711.05101_opening.txt\",\n      \"original_pdf_sha256\": \"9d5af48931e773d6983d167e15c06ef594475b751c1f263827f5ed3c7a00d0d8\",\n      \"scope\": \"exact user-authorized original; no citation/recommendation traversal\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"queries\": [\n    \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2201.02177\",\n    \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2210.01117\",\n    \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2310.06110\",\n    \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.02390\",\n    \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2301.05217\",\n    \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2303.06173\",\n    \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1912.02292\",\n    \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1711.05101\"\n  ],\n  \"scope_limitation\": \"The strategy limits consultation to eight authorized originals. The requested minimum of 25 references cannot be met without broadening that contract; unrelated retrieved records were excluded, not cited to fill a quota.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '32,49p'; nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '74,119p'; nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '192,265p'; cat references.bib\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '32,49p'; nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '74,119p'; nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '192,265p'; cat references.bib\"","aggregated_output":"    32\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n    33\t\\centering\n    34\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/study_overview.png}\n    35\t\\caption{Study design and evidence interpretation. Width varies within a fixed modular-addition network. The studied configuration is compared with a fixed reference, a zero-decay control, and initial-global-norm matching. Fitting, observed generalization, right censoring, and unavailable records have distinct meanings; none independently certifies scientific qualification.}\n    36\t\\label{fig:overview}\n    37\t\\end{figure}\n    38\t\n    39\t\\section{Related Work}\n    40\t\\paragraph{Algorithmic generalization and internal progress.}\n    41\t\\citet{Power2022GrokkingGB} established small algorithmically generated datasets as a setting for generalization well after training-set fitting. \\citet{Nanda2023Progress} reverse-engineered a Fourier-based algorithm in small Transformers trained on modular addition and developed progress measures based on that computation. Our network instead sums learned input embeddings before a GELU and a linear readout. We measure threshold onsets and endpoint behavior, not Fourier circuits or representation-specific progress. Consequently, architectural or mechanistic conclusions from those Transformers are not evidence for the internal computation of the present network.\n    42\t\n    43\t\\paragraph{Norm, efficiency, and feature learning.}\n    44\t\\citet{Liu2022OmnigrokGB} relate grokking to training and test loss landscapes as a function of weight norm. \\citet{Varma2023ExplainingGT} explain delayed generalization through competition between memorizing and generalizing circuits with different norm efficiency and learning speed. \\citet{Kumar2023GrokkingAT} study a lazy-to-rich transition, including polynomial regression in which delayed generalization occurs without weight decay. These accounts motivate different diagnostics: initial norm, efficiency of task-relevant computations, and evolving features are not interchangeable quantities. Our norm-rescaling and zero-decay controls address limited observable consequences. They do not measure circuit efficiency, kernel alignment, or the onset of feature learning, and cannot select among these explanations.\n    45\t\n    46\t\\paragraph{Capacity and duration.}\n    47\t\\citet{Davies2023Unifying} propose a pattern-learning-speed framework connecting grokking and double descent, including changes in model capacity. \\citet{Nakkiran2020Double} show that model size and training duration can each produce nonmonotone test behavior. These works motivate joint attention to width and observation horizon, rather than an assumption that larger networks must generalize better at every endpoint. Our narrow width sweep is not an estimate of a double-descent curve. Finally, AdamW decouples weight decay from the adaptive loss-gradient update \\citep{Loshchilov2019AdamW}. Matching the nominal decay factor is therefore a useful optimizer description, not proof of equal effective regularization or equal post-fit exposure.\n    48\t\n    49\t\\section{Method}\n    74\t\\begin{itemize}\n    75\t\\item \\textbf{Studied:} the common configuration above.\n    76\t\\item \\textbf{Reference:} $\\eta=0.001$, $\\lambda=1.0$, $m=1.0$, with the same width, seed, split, and horizon. Because both learning rate and initialization differ, this is a configuration comparison, not a component-specific test.\n    77\t\\item \\textbf{No decay:} the studied configuration with $\\lambda=0$. This isolates removal of decay within the measured setting, not its necessity for all delayed generalization.\n    78\t\\item \\textbf{Norm matched:} the studied initialization is rescaled so its global Euclidean parameter norm equals that of the same-seed width-128 initialization. Learning rate and decay are unchanged. This matches one scalar, not capacity, layerwise geometry, or initial function.\n    79\t\\end{itemize}\n    80\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L795]]\n    81\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L821]]\n    82\tAt width 128 the norm-matching target is the original model itself. The retained initial/final state hashes and scientific metric trajectories match the studied condition there. This duplicate supplies neither an independent replication nor an additional delayed event.\n    83\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    84\t\n    85\t\\subsection{Training Protocol and Event Definitions}\n    86\tDevelopment observations use seeds 101 and 211 and a horizon of 6,000 updates. Final observations use seeds 60613, 70717, and 80821 with a planned horizon of 24,000 updates. Accuracy is recorded at initialization and every 100 updates. All four conditions in a retained cell use the same split. Distinct development/final pools prevent exact input overlap across those resources, but the longer final horizon also changes alongside the seeds and data. No paired duration-only contrast is available.\n    87\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    88\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    89\t\n    90\tFor a checkpoint accuracy sequence $A(t)$, define a sustained threshold onset as\n    91\t\\begin{equation}\n    92\t T_q[A]=\\min\\{t_j:\\ A(t_j),A(t_{j+1}),A(t_{j+2})\\geq q\\},\n    93\t \\qquad\n    94\t \\fit=T_{0.99}[A_{\\mathrm{train}}],\\quad\n    95\t \\gen=T_{0.95}[A_{\\mathrm{eval}}].\n    96\t \\label{eq:onset}\n    97\t\\end{equation}\n    98\tThe onset is the first checkpoint in the qualifying triplet, not the confirmation time. When both onsets exist, the delay is $\\max(0,\\gen-\\fit)$. A delayed event requires a delay of at least 1,000 updates. If either onset is absent, the evaluator records censoring and a normalized capped-delay value of one. This last convention is a reporting functional, not an observed event time. Three consecutive checkpoints suppress single-checkpoint spikes but do not guarantee persistent accuracy after the qualifying window. We retain the distinction between threshold-defined delayed generalization and a mechanistically verified transition.\n    99\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L1001]]\n   100\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   101\t\n   102\t\\subsection{Reporting, Missingness, and Uncertainty}\n   103\tThe unit of replication is the seed, not the evaluation example, checkpoint, or matched condition. We report all retained seed values and descriptive sample standard deviations where appropriate. Paired differences use only seeds shared by the compared widths. We do not replace the planned widest-minus-narrowest final contrast with an intermediate-minus-narrowest contrast without explicitly naming that change in estimand. Missing width records have no performance outcome; they are not measured losses, censored trajectories, or zero-accuracy models.\n   104\t\n   105\tFor transparency, we also give exact binomial intervals for delayed-event counts conditional on retained records. These intervals describe what a hypothetical independent-seed sampling model would allow; the small sample and unknown missingness preclude a population interpretation. No inferential test uses examples or checkpoints as extra replications. Failed final qualification applies independently of these descriptive calculations. The final summary supplies no criterion-specific diagnostics, so neither the observed missingness nor the retained controls can certify validity or identify the cause of non-qualification.\n   106\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/limitations]]\n   107\t% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#/cells]]\n   108\t\n   109\t\\section{Results}\n   110\t\\subsection{Evidence Coverage and Scientific Qualification}\n   111\tTable~\\ref{tab:coverage} gives the full final observation pattern for the studied condition. Only five of nine planned width--seed cells are retained: three at width 32, two at width 128, and none at width 256. All retained cells contain complete-horizon trajectories for the matched conditions. At width 32, failure to observe the sustained generalization threshold is right censoring at the recorded horizon. At width 256, absence of trajectories provides no information about onset or endpoint accuracy. The missing width-128 seed is likewise unavailable, not evidence against generalization.\n   112\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   113\t\n   114\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   115\t\\centering\n   116\t\\caption{Final coverage for the studied condition. C: recorded trajectory, generalization onset censored at 24,000 updates; D: recorded delayed event; M: missing record. A missing cell is not assigned an accuracy or a censoring time. All conditions in each retained cell were recorded.}\n   117\t\\label{tab:coverage}\n   118\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n   119\t\\toprule\n   192\t% @evidence[[derived/observations.json#/rows]]\n   193\t\n   194\tGlobal initial-norm matching does not eliminate the short-horizon narrow-versus-intermediate ordering: width-32 endpoints are 70.510\\% and 56.164\\%, compared with 31.934\\% and 26.727\\% at width 128. This challenges the limited proposal that matching this scalar alone would remove that observed ordering. It does not isolate a causal norm mediator. The intervention changes all nonzero initial parameter magnitudes together, while width still changes capacity and feature geometry. At width 128 it makes no change at all, so its delayed events duplicate the studied observations rather than independently confirming a norm explanation.\n   195\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   196\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   197\t\n   198\tThe retained final reference controls also do not cross the sustained held-out threshold. Their endpoints range from 54.570\\% to 65.037\\% at width 32 and from 80.659\\% to 84.272\\% at width 128. These are matched reference-configuration observations, not an attribution to initialization or learning rate alone. Together the controls narrow interpretation: they show that early fitting can coexist with poor held-out endpoints, and that scalar norm matching does not make width interchangeable. They do not reveal why delayed generalization occurs in the two retained intermediate-width trajectories.\n   199\t% @evidence[[derived/observations.json#/rows]]\n   200\t\n   201\t\\subsection{Endpoint Norms and Losses Bound a Scalar-Clock Interpretation}\n   202\tAccuracy-based fitting is not equivalent to convergence of the training objective. Every retained final studied trajectory ends at perfect training accuracy, but the corresponding cross-entropy losses differ substantially across widths (Table~\\ref{tab:normloss}). The narrow models retain nonzero training loss even after their accuracy threshold has been exceeded; the intermediate-width models have much smaller final training losses. A statement about the time to classify the training set therefore should not be silently replaced by a statement about optimization completion, margin growth, or the time at which regularization becomes dominant.\n   203\t% @evidence[[derived/observations.json#/rows]]\n   204\t\n   205\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   206\t\\centering\n   207\t\\caption{Endpoint diagnostics for all retained final studied trajectories. Norms are global Euclidean parameter norms; losses are cross-entropy at 24,000 updates. All listed endpoint training accuracies equal one. Net norm increases coexist with both observed and censored held-out onsets. No width-256 diagnostic is available.}\n   208\t\\label{tab:normloss}\n   209\t\\small\n   210\t\\begin{tabular}{rrrrrr}\n   211\t\\toprule\n   212\tSeed & Width & Initial norm & Final norm & Train loss & Held-out loss \\\\\n   213\t\\midrule\n   214\t60613 & 32 & 40.45 & 93.97 & 0.202 & 0.935 \\\\\n   215\t60613 & 128 & 79.18 & 128.10 & 5.6e-05 & 0.110 \\\\\n   216\t70717 & 32 & 40.31 & 94.18 & 0.178 & 0.703 \\\\\n   217\t70717 & 128 & 79.34 & 128.31 & 5.78e-05 & 0.137 \\\\\n   218\t80821 & 32 & 39.76 & 94.44 & 0.199 & 1.064 \\\\\n   219\t\\bottomrule\n   220\t\\end{tabular}\n   221\t% @evidence[[derived/norm_loss_records.tex]]\n   222\t\\end{table}\n   223\t\n   224\tGlobal norms increase between initialization and the final checkpoint in every retained studied record, including the delayed-event records. Thus, a positive weight-decay coefficient cannot be read as evidence of a net decline in the measured total norm. These endpoint comparisons do not establish monotonic norm growth during training, nor do they refute explanations based on relative circuit efficiency or trajectories through a loss landscape. A generalizing computation could become more efficient while the total norm still rises. The diagnostics instead expose the gap between an optimizer-level shrinkage term and a measured model-level explanation: the adaptive loss-gradient update and the decay update act together, while our evidence does not isolate their contributions to the learned computation.\n   225\t% @evidence[[derived/observations.json#/rows]]\n   226\t\n   227\t\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n   228\t\\subsection{A Conditional Timescale Distinction, Not a Size Law}\n   229\tThe cleanest interpretation is that training fit and useful generalization are separate empirical targets. In the complete short-horizon width comparisons, fitting accelerates while endpoint generalization does not improve monotonically. In the incomplete final comparisons, observed intermediate-width events are compatible with a long separation after fitting. These observations support the working explanation at a descriptive level. They do not tell us whether a wider model changes the formation, competition, or efficiency of task-relevant computations. A claim about any of those mechanisms would need corresponding measurements rather than a reinterpretation of accuracy alone.\n   230\t\n   231\tThe development/final contrast illustrates why observation horizon must be part of a statement about width. It does not show that extending training caused a reversal of the width ranking, since data and seeds also changed. Likewise, the fact that both observed delayed events occur at the intermediate width does not locate an optimal width: the largest width is missing entirely in the final data. Treating absence of the largest models as a negative outcome would manufacture the very comparison needed for that conclusion.\n   232\t\n   233\tThe no-decay results should be interpreted within the same boundary. AdamW applies a nominal multiplicative shrinkage factor of $1-\\eta\\lambda$ per update, alongside an adaptive gradient update. This factor is not a complete dynamical clock. Models may fit at different times, have different gradient histories, and accumulate different post-fit exposure even when this factor agrees. No positive-decay factorial grid, clock-collapse analysis, or direct efficiency measurement was completed in the retained evidence. A nominal optimizer relationship cannot substitute for those missing tests.\n   234\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L833]]\n   235\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs]]\n   236\t\n   237\t\\subsection{Validity, Selection, and Missing Information}\n   238\tThe final qualification failure is a substantive boundary on interpretation. No supplied criterion-level diagnosis establishes that the failure was merely insufficient performance, merely statistical uncertainty, or a protocol defect. Reporting the retained trajectories does not alter that status. Their source-linked availability permits inspection and reproduction of descriptive calculations; it does not certify all scientific conclusions one might wish to draw from them. The same caution applies to provenance hashes: matching source identity helps locate the evaluated implementation, but the readable implementation and actual observations, not the hash alone, support its substantive description.\n   239\t\n   240\tDevelopment observations were used to select the common configuration. Their apparent advantages over the fixed reference are therefore not an unbiased assessment of a newly chosen setting. The final data provide a separate evaluation, but missing cells prevent the planned complete-width contrasts, and its non-qualification prevents presenting it as a successful confirmation. Incomplete alternative configurations are preserved in the supplement rather than converted into measured losses or a retrospective success narrative.\n   241\t\n   242\tUncertainty is limited by the number of independent seeds, not by the number of labeled pairs. Seed-specific splits and coupled initialization make pairing useful, but also mean that each seed jointly varies multiple stochastic ingredients. We do not separately identify split uncertainty and initialization uncertainty. The retained records do not justify an assumption that missing cells are random. Consequently, complete-case endpoint differences, exact event-count intervals, and descriptive standard deviations must remain conditional summaries, not missing-data corrections or population guarantees.\n   243\t\n   244\tThe setting is also narrow: a single modular operation and modulus, a particular shallow architecture, fixed full-batch optimization, and a fixed observation rule. We did not measure random-label controls, frozen features, circuit ablations, additional moduli, or transfer to other tasks. No claim extends to those unmeasured conditions. Even within this task, three-checkpoint onset is an operational marker rather than proof of a permanent or abrupt algorithmic change. The conclusion concerns the recorded thresholds and endpoints, not an unobserved internal transition.\n   245\t\n   246\t\\section{Conclusion}\n   247\tHidden width and delayed generalization cannot be summarized by training-fit speed alone. In the available modular-addition comparisons, increasing width accelerates sustained fitting while held-out accuracy remains nonmonotone at the short horizon. The retained longer-horizon data contain delayed events at the intermediate width and censored narrow-width trajectories, but omit all largest-width records and one intermediate-width seed. These findings support a bounded separation between fitting and finite-horizon generalization, not a qualified scaling law.\n   248\t\n   249\tThe controls make that boundary more informative. Removing decay preserves rapid fitting without comparable held-out performance in the measured setting; matching initial global norm does not remove the observed development ordering and is exactly redundant at the matching width. Neither result identifies the mechanism of delayed generalization. Most importantly, the final evaluation failed scientific qualification, and its cause remains unspecified. A source-linked descriptive account is therefore the appropriate endpoint of this evidence: report the observed comparisons, distinguish censoring from missingness, and leave broad width effects and causal explanations unresolved.\n   250\t\n   251\t\\FloatBarrier\n   252\t\\clearpage\n   253\t\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n   254\t\\bibliography{references}\n   255\t\\clearpage\n   256\t\\appendix\n   257\t\\section{Supplementary Information}\n   258\t\n   259\t\\subsection{Inspectable Implementation and Data Resources}\n   260\t\\label{sec:source}\n   261\tThe included training implementation is \\path{source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py}. Architecture, initialization coupling, norm matching, and optimizer construction appear at lines 781--835; checkpoint measurement and local controls at lines 869--913; sustained-onset extraction at lines 1001--1016; and paired-width contrasts at lines 1074--1093. Its SHA-256 equals the final evaluator digest recorded in the evidence registry. This exact source check supplements, rather than replaces, inspection of the readable training code. Source and resource hashes are recorded in \\path{derived/source_manifest.json}. The local controller snapshot is in \\path{source_evidence/controllers/studied/}; its readable response specifies the common settings and agrees with every retained studied configuration. We do not claim an independent reconstruction of the complete frozen source tree from a tree digest.\n   262\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L781]]\n   263\t% @evidence[[derived/source_manifest.json]]\n   264\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluator_sha256]]\n   265\t\n@misc{Power2022GrokkingGB,\n  author = {Alethea Power and Yuri Burda and Harri Edwards and Igor Babuschkin and Vedant Misra},\n  title = {{Grokking: Generalization Beyond Overfitting on Small Algorithmic Datasets}},\n  year = {2022},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2201.02177},\n  eprint = {2201.02177},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv}\n}\n\n@misc{Liu2022OmnigrokGB,\n  author = {Ziming Liu and Eric J. Michaud and Max Tegmark},\n  title = {{Omnigrok: Grokking Beyond Algorithmic Data}},\n  year = {2023},\n  howpublished = {International Conference on Learning Representations},\n  eprint = {2210.01117},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv}\n}\n\n@misc{Kumar2023GrokkingAT,\n  author = {Tanishq Kumar and Blake Bordelon and Samuel J. Gershman and Cengiz Pehlevan},\n  title = {{Grokking as the Transition from Lazy to Rich Training Dynamics}},\n  year = {2024},\n  howpublished = {International Conference on Learning Representations},\n  eprint = {2310.06110},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv}\n}\n\n@misc{Varma2023ExplainingGT,\n  author = {Vikrant Varma and Rohin Shah and Zachary Kenton and J{\\'a}nos Kram{\\'a}r and Ramana Kumar},\n  title = {{Explaining Grokking Through Circuit Efficiency}},\n  year = {2023},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2309.02390},\n  eprint = {2309.02390},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv}\n}\n\n@misc{Nanda2023Progress,\n  author = {Neel Nanda and Lawrence Chan and Tom Lieberum and Jess Smith and Jacob Steinhardt},\n  title = {{Progress Measures for Grokking via Mechanistic Interpretability}},\n  year = {2023},\n  howpublished = {International Conference on Learning Representations},\n  eprint = {2301.05217},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv}\n}\n\n@misc{Davies2023Unifying,\n  author = {Xander Davies and Lauro Langosco and David Krueger},\n  title = {{Unifying Grokking and Double Descent}},\n  year = {2023},\n  howpublished = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2303.06173},\n  eprint = {2303.06173},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv}\n}\n\n@misc{Nakkiran2020Double,\n  author = {Preetum Nakkiran and Gal Kaplun and Yamini Bansal and Tristan Yang and Boaz Barak and Ilya Sutskever},\n  title = {{Deep Double Descent: Where Bigger Models and More Data Hurt}},\n  year = {2020},\n  howpublished = {International Conference on Learning Representations},\n  eprint = {1912.02292},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv}\n}\n\n@misc{Loshchilov2019AdamW,\n  author = {Ilya Loshchilov and Frank Hutter},\n  title = {{Decoupled Weight Decay Regularization}},\n  year = {2019},\n  howpublished = {International Conference on Learning Representations},\n  eprint = {1711.05101},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv}\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
