me. All conditions in each retained cell were recorded.}\n   117\t\\label{tab:coverage}\n   118\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n   207\t\\caption{Endpoint diagnostics for all retained final studied trajectories. Norms are global Euclidean parameter norms; losses are cross-entropy at 24,000 updates. All listed endpoint training accuracies equal one. Net norm increases coexist with both observed and censored held-out onsets. No width-256 diagnostic is available.}\n   208\t\\label{tab:normloss}\n   209\t\\small\n   210\t\\begin{tabular}{rrrrrr}\n   211\t\\toprule\n   212\tSeed & Width & Initial norm & Final norm & Train loss & Held-out loss \\\\\n   213\t\\midrule\n   214\t60613 & 32 & 40.45 & 93.97 & 0.202 & 0.935 \\\\\n   215\t60613 & 128 & 79.18 & 128.10 & 5.6e-05 & 0.110 \\\\\n   216\t70717 & 32 & 40.31 & 94.18 & 0.178 & 0.703 \\\\\n   217\t70717 & 128 & 79.34 & 128.31 & 5.78e-05 & 0.137 \\\\\n   218\t80821 & 32 & 39.76 & 94.44 & 0.199 & 1.064 \\\\\n   219\t\\bottomrule\n   220\t\\end{tabular}\n   221\t% @evidence[[derived/norm_loss_records.tex]]\n   222\t\\end{table}\n   223\t\n   224\tGlobal norms increase between initialization and the final checkpoint in every retained studied record, including the delayed-event records. Thus, a positive weight-decay coefficient cannot be read as evidence of a net decline in the measured total norm. These endpoint comparisons do not establish monotonic norm growth during training, nor do they refute explanations based on relative circuit efficiency or trajectories through a loss landscape. A generalizing computation could become more efficient while the total norm still rises. The diagnostics instead expose the gap between an optimizer-level shrinkage term and a measured model-level explanation: the adaptive loss-gradient update and the decay update act together, while our evidence does not isolate their contributions to the learned computation.\n   225\t% @evidence[[derived/observations.json#/rows]]\n   226\t\n   227\t\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n   228\t\\subsection{A Conditional Timescale Distinction, Not a Size Law}\n   229\tThe cleanest interpretation is that training fit and useful generalization are separate empirical targets. In the complete short-horizon width comparisons, fitting accelerates while endpoint generalization does not improve monotonically. In the incomplete final comparisons, observed intermediate-width events are compatible with a long separation after fitting. These observations support the working explanation at a descriptive level. They do not tell us whether a wider model changes the formation, competition, or efficiency of task-relevant computations. A claim about any of those mechanisms would need corresponding measurements rather than a reinterpretation of accuracy alone.\n   230\t\n   231\tThe development/final contrast illustrates why observation horizon must be part of a statement about width. It does not show that extending training caused a reversal of the width ranking, since data and seeds also changed. Likewise, the fact that both observed delayed events occur at the intermediate width does not locate an optimal width: the largest width is missing entirely in the final data. Treating absence of the largest models as a negative outcome would manufacture the very comparison needed for that conclusion.\n   232\t\n   233\tThe no-decay results should be interpreted within the same boundary. AdamW applies a nominal multiplicative shrinkage factor of $1-\\eta\\lambda$ per update, alongside an adaptive gradient update. This factor is not a complete dynamical clock. Models may fit at different times, have different gradient histories, and accumulate different post-fit exposure even when this factor agrees. No positive-decay factorial grid, clock-collapse analysis, or direct efficiency measurement was completed in the retained evidence. A nominal optimizer relationship cannot substitute for those missing tests.\n   234\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L833]]\n   235\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs]]\n   236\t\n   237\t\\subsection{Validity, Selection, and Missing Information}\n   238\tThe final qualification failure is a substantive boundary on interpretation. No supplied criterion-level diagnosis establishes that the failure was merely insufficient performance, merely statistical uncertainty, or a protocol defect. Reporting the retained trajectories does not alter that status. Their source-linked availability permits inspection and reproduction of descriptive calculations; it does not certify all scientific conclusions one might wish to draw from them. The same caution applies to provenance hashes: matching source identity helps locate the evaluated implementation, but the readable implementation and actual observations, not the hash alone, support its substantive description.\n   239\t\n   240\tDevelopment observations were used to select the common configuration. Their apparent advantages over the fixed reference are therefore not an unbiased assessment of a newly chosen setting. The final data provide a separate evaluation, but missing cells prevent the planned complete-width contrasts, and its non-qualification prevents presenting it as a successful confirmation. Incomplete alternative configurations are preserved in the supplement rather than converted into measured losses or a retrospective success narrative.\n   241\t\n   242\tUncertainty is limited by the number of independent seeds, not by the number of labeled pairs. Seed-specific splits and coupled initialization make pairing useful, but also mean that each seed jointly varies multiple stochastic ingredients. We do not separately identify split uncertainty and initialization uncertainty. The retained records do not justify an assumption that missing cells are random. Consequently, complete-case endpoint differences, exact event-count intervals, and descriptive standard deviations must remain conditional summaries, not missing-data corrections or population guarantees.\n   243\t\n   244\tThe setting is also narrow: a single modular operation and modulus, a particular shallow architecture, fixed full-batch optimization, and a fixed observation rule. We did not measure random-label controls, frozen features, circuit ablations, additional moduli, or transfer to other tasks. No claim extends to those unmeasured conditions. Even within this task, three-checkpoint onset is an operational marker rather than proof of a permanent or abrupt algorithmic change. The conclusion concerns the recorded thresholds and endpoints, not an unobserved internal transition.\n   245\t\n   246\t\\section{Conclusion}\n   247\tHidden width and delayed generalization cannot be summarized by training-fit speed alone. In the available modular-addition comparisons, increasing width accelerates sustained fitting while held-out accuracy remains nonmonotone at the short horizon. The retained longer-horizon data contain delayed events at the intermediate width and censored narrow-width trajectories, but omit all largest-width records and one intermediate-width seed. These findings support a bounded separation between fitting and finite-horizon generalization, not a qualified scaling law.\n   248\t\n   249\tThe controls make that boundary more informative. Removing decay preserves rapid fitting without comparable held-out performance in the measured setting; matching initial global norm does not remove the observed development ordering and is exactly redundant at the matching width. Neither result identifies the mechanism of delayed generalization. Most importantly, the final evaluation failed scientific qualification, and its cause remains unspecified. A source-linked descriptive account is therefore the appropriate endpoint of this evidence: report the observed comparisons, distinguish censoring from missingness, and leave broad width effects and causal explanations unresolved.\n   309\t\n   310\tThe full machine-readable record \\path{derived/observations.json} also lists initial and final global norms, parameter counts, initial/final state hashes, updates, and recorded condition durations. It is deterministically extracted from the immutable evidence registry rather than a new evaluation. At width 128, comparison of initial and final hashes and the non-wall-time checkpoint metrics confirms the identity of the studied and norm-matched observations. Their runtime measurements need not coincide, so this identity concerns model states and scientific metrics, not elapsed time.\n   311\t% @evidence[[derived/observations.json#/rows]]\n   312\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   313\t\n   314\t\\subsection{Selection Sensitivity and Incomplete Settings}\n   315\tThe development evidence contains related common configurations with learning-rate/decay/initialization triples $(0.003,1.0,1.0)$, $(0.003,1.0,0.5)$, and $(0.006,0.5,0.5)$. These are sensitivity conditions, not independent scientific replications of the final comparison. The first two differ in initialization at fixed learning rate and decay. The last differs in learning rate and decay while preserving the nominal product $\\eta\\lambda$ relative to the studied condition. Equal products do not imply equal adaptive updates or effective regularization. Their full observations remain in the registry, and the controller snapshots are supplied with the code.\n   316\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs/0/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   317\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs/1/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   318\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs/2/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   319\t\n   320\tThe faster-update configuration has incomplete recorded coverage. A missing record cannot be used to claim that its accuracy was zero or that it lost a measured comparison. Its reported aggregate execution-related rates do not identify a cause of scientific failure. We do not promote its nominal shrinkage relationship into a completed positive-decay factorial intervention, a post-fit exposure match, or a clock-collapse result. The measured reference conditions within retained cells provide the appropriate local baseline; no unavailable external baseline is reconstructed from a selection score.\n   321\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs/2]]\n   322\t\n   323\t\\subsection{Censoring, Denominators, and Uncertainty}\n   324\tThe normalized capped delay is $r=\\max(0,\\gen-\\fit)/H$ when both onsets are observed, and $r=1$ otherwise. The observed width-128 final values are $17300/24000$ and $19000/24000$. Assigning the same cap to a missing cell in an aggregate is a weighting convention, not evidence that its generalization was censored. Likewise, a missing final endpoint receives no measured accuracy in our per-seed tables or figures.\n   325\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L1008]]\n   326\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   327\t\n   328\tThe supplied final design-weighted summaries are delayed-event fraction $0.222222$, endpoint-accuracy functional $0.488133$, and normalized capped-delay functional $0.945833$. The first uses nine planned cells, the second includes missing-cell zero weighting, and the third assigns missing cells the cap. They are not, respectively, a population event probability, the mean accuracy of the five observed models, or an observed mean generalization time. For comparison, the descriptive mean of the five retained studied endpoints is $0.878640$. We retain these semantics for auditability, not as stronger headline results.\n   329\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.metric.grokking_incidence]]\n   330\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.metric.mean_final_accuracy]]\n   331\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.metric.restricted_mean_delay]]\n   332\t% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#/final_observed_endpoint_mean]]\n   333\t\n   334\tThe two-sided exact binomial intervals use beta quantiles: for $k$ events among $n$ retained seeds, the lower endpoint is zero for $k=0$ and otherwise $B^{-1}_{k,n-k+1}(0.025)$; the upper endpoint is one for $k=n$ and otherwise $B^{-1}_{k+1,n-k}(0.975)$. This calculation is exploratory and conditional on retained records. It neither assumes that unavailable records are observed non-events nor establishes that missingness is ignorable. No survival-model estimate, significance test, or mechanistic confidence interval is reported.\n   335\t% @evidence[[scripts/build_artifacts.py]]\n   336\t% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#/cells]]\n   337\t\n   338\t\\subsection{Reproducibility}\n   339\tThe recorded training environment requires NumPy 2.1.3 and PyTorch 2.5.1+cpu, with deterministic algorithms and at most two CPU threads; the worker uses one inter-op thread. The source requirement file is included. All development seeds are 101 and 211; all final seeds are 60613, 70717, and 80821. These seeds initialize both the recorded partitions and the coupled model initialization, rather than selecting width-dependent settings.\n   340\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/requirements.txt]]\n   341\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L765]]\n   342\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L781]]\n   343\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   344\t\n   345\t\\path{scripts/build_artifacts.py} generates both quantitative figures, the complete final table, the extracted records, and descriptive statistics. It recomputes threshold onsets from the stored curves and checks equality against the recorded event fields. Its output PDFs are used in the manuscript; PNGs are exported from the same source. Table~\\ref{tab:development} is a rounded display of \\path{derived/summary.json}; Table~\\ref{tab:coverage} is the missingness pattern in the same evidence. The overview is rendered separately by the designated concept-figure workflow, not inferred from invented learning curves.\n   346\t% @evidence[[scripts/build_artifacts.py]]\n   347\t% @evidence[[figure_manifest.json]]\n   348\t\n   349\tThe accompanying \\path{REPRODUCIBILITY.md} gives environment setup, analysis, compilation, and training commands. Rebuilding the descriptive artifacts does not launch training. Recorded development evaluation durations are approximately 981 and 754 seconds for the two selected-seed evaluations; final recorded totals are approximately 3,199, 2,794, and 3,488 seconds by seed. The final totals include incomplete coverage and overhead, and cannot estimate the time required for a complete final grid on other hardware. No new training or evaluation was run during manuscript construction.\n   350\t% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#/runtime_by_scope_seconds]]\n   351\t\n   352\tOnly the exact authorized original literature was consulted, without following citation networks or recommendation pages. Supplied retrieval metadata included off-scope records, which were excluded rather than used to pad the bibliography. No prohibited work was intentionally sought or identified, and no known prohibited encounter is asserted.\n   353\t% @evidence[[references_provenance.json]]\n   354\t\n   355\t\\subsection{LLM Usage}\n   356\tLLMs were used to formulate the documented optimization hypotheses, edit the common-configuration controllers, synthesize the supplied evaluation artifacts into the present scientific framing, write analysis and plotting code, consult the opening portions of the authorized original references, draft the manuscript, and check claim-to-evidence consistency. Training and numerical measurements were produced by the supplied CPU evaluator, not by language-model estimation. The manuscript-writing process did not execute additional training or final evaluations. Provenance sidecars and claim mappings record the evidence trail but are not independent certification of the conclusions. The failure of final scientific qualification remains unchanged by manuscript preparation or validation.\n   357\t\n   358\t\\end{document}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
