{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '224,245p' paper.tex; sed -n '265,310p' paper.tex; sed -n '1,14p' global_memory_summary.md; python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nwith open('paper_strategy.json') as stream: strategy=json.load(stream)\nfor key in ['central_question','notes_for_writer','staged_input_impact','edit_framing_decision']:\n    print(key+':', json.dumps(strategy[key],ensure_ascii=False))\nwith open('claims_grounding.json') as stream: grounding=json.load(stream)\nfor index,claim in enumerate(grounding['claims'],1):\n    print(str(index)+': '+claim['claim'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '224,245p' paper.tex; sed -n '265,310p' paper.tex; sed -n '1,14p' global_memory_summary.md; python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nwith open('paper_strategy.json') as stream: strategy=json.load(stream)\nfor key in ['central_question','notes_for_writer','staged_input_impact','edit_framing_decision']:\n    print(key+':', json.dumps(strategy[key],ensure_ascii=False))\nwith open('claims_grounding.json') as stream: grounding=json.load(stream)\nfor index,claim in enumerate(grounding['claims'],1):\n    print(str(index)+': '+claim['claim'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"Global norms increase between initialization and the final checkpoint in every retained studied record, including the delayed-event records. Thus, a positive weight-decay coefficient cannot be read as evidence of a net decline in the measured total norm. These endpoint comparisons do not establish monotonic norm growth during training, nor do they refute explanations based on relative circuit efficiency or trajectories through a loss landscape. A generalizing computation could become more efficient while the total norm still rises. The diagnostics instead expose the gap between an optimizer-level shrinkage term and a measured model-level explanation: the adaptive loss-gradient update and the decay update act together, while our evidence does not isolate their contributions to the learned computation.\n% @evidence[[derived/observations.json#/rows]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\subsection{A Conditional Timescale Distinction, Not a Size Law}\nThe cleanest interpretation is that training fit and useful generalization are separate empirical targets. In the complete short-horizon width comparisons, fitting accelerates while endpoint generalization does not improve monotonically. In the incomplete final comparisons, observed intermediate-width events are compatible with a long separation after fitting. These observations support the working explanation at a descriptive level. They do not tell us whether a wider model changes the formation, competition, or efficiency of task-relevant computations. A claim about any of those mechanisms would need corresponding measurements rather than a reinterpretation of accuracy alone.\n\nThe development/final contrast illustrates why observation horizon must be part of a statement about width. It does not show that extending training caused a reversal of the width ranking, since data and seeds also changed. Likewise, the fact that both observed delayed events occur at the intermediate width does not locate an optimal width: the largest width is missing entirely in the final data. Treating absence of the largest models as a negative outcome would manufacture the very comparison needed for that conclusion.\n\nThe no-decay results should be interpreted within the same boundary. AdamW applies a nominal multiplicative shrinkage factor of $1-\\eta\\lambda$ per update, alongside an adaptive gradient update. This factor is not a complete dynamical clock. Models may fit at different times, have different gradient histories, and accumulate different post-fit exposure even when this factor agrees. No positive-decay factorial grid, clock-collapse analysis, or direct efficiency measurement was completed in the retained evidence. A nominal optimizer relationship cannot substitute for those missing tests.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L833]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs]]\n\n\\subsection{Validity, Selection, and Missing Information}\nThe final qualification failure is a substantive boundary on interpretation. No supplied criterion-level diagnosis establishes that the failure was merely insufficient performance, merely statistical uncertainty, or a protocol defect. Reporting the retained trajectories does not alter that status. Their source-linked availability permits inspection and reproduction of descriptive calculations; it does not certify all scientific conclusions one might wish to draw from them. The same caution applies to provenance hashes: matching source identity helps locate the evaluated implementation, but the readable implementation and actual observations, not the hash alone, support its substantive description.\n\nDevelopment observations were used to select the common configuration. Their apparent advantages over the fixed reference are therefore not an unbiased assessment of a newly chosen setting. The final data provide a separate evaluation, but missing cells prevent the planned complete-width contrasts, and its non-qualification prevents presenting it as a successful confirmation. Incomplete alternative configurations are preserved in the supplement rather than converted into measured losses or a retrospective success narrative.\n\nUncertainty is limited by the number of independent seeds, not by the number of labeled pairs. Seed-specific splits and coupled initialization make pairing useful, but also mean that each seed jointly varies multiple stochastic ingredients. We do not separately identify split uncertainty and initialization uncertainty. The retained records do not justify an assumption that missing cells are random. Consequently, complete-case endpoint differences, exact event-count intervals, and descriptive standard deviations must remain conditional summaries, not missing-data corrections or population guarantees.\n\nThe setting is also narrow: a single modular operation and modulus, a particular shallow architecture, fixed full-batch optimization, and a fixed observation rule. We did not measure random-label controls, frozen features, circuit ablations, additional moduli, or transfer to other tasks. No claim extends to those unmeasured conditions. Even within this task, three-checkpoint onset is an operational marker rather than proof of a permanent or abrupt algorithmic change. The conclusion concerns the recorded thresholds and endpoints, not an unobserved internal transition.\n\n\nThe two data resources are \\path{source_evidence/task/resources/search/dataset.json} and \\path{source_evidence/task/resources/final/dataset.json}. They include the explicit integer triples, source-row identities, seed-specific train/evaluation indices, and split digests. All labels are verified by modular addition in the supplied evaluator. No third-party dataset download, account, license, or external snapshot is needed. Although the task domain is exhaustively enumerable, training and evaluation use the resource pools and their recorded splits, not an assumed random partition of the complete domain at each width. The partition is shared across widths within a seed.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L696]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/resources/search/dataset.json]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/resources/final/dataset.json]]\n\nThe reference uses the same seeded Gaussian draws as the studied model but a different initialization multiplier and learning rate. The norm-matched control rescales every parameter by the ratio of the same-seed width-128 norm to the current norm. Biases initially equal zero, so this is a global scaling of the nonzero embedding and readout tensors. It does not make individual layer norms equal across widths. Full-batch training leaves the optimizer's other settings at the pinned library defaults, with \\texttt{foreach=False}; we do not infer additional tuning of unspecified optimizer parameters.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L795]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L821]]\n\n\\subsection{Complete Retained Final Comparisons}\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{All retained final condition endpoints and onsets. S: studied; R: reference; Z: no decay; N: norm matched. Onset values are updates. The $>24000$ notation means no sustained held-out onset was recorded; it is not an extrapolated event time. All listed training onsets are observed. Missing width--seed cells are given separately in Table~\\ref{tab:coverage}, not inserted as zero-valued rows.}\n\\label{tab:allfinal}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{rrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nSeed & Width & Arm & Accuracy (\\%) & Fit & Onset \\\\\n\\midrule\n60613 & 32 & S & 80.925 & 2600 & $>24000$ \\\\\n60613 & 32 & R & 59.352 & 9100 & $>24000$ \\\\\n60613 & 32 & N & 67.747 & 3400 & $>24000$ \\\\\n60613 & 32 & Z & 0.478 & 1600 & $>24000$ \\\\\n60613 & 128 & S & 98.353 & 300 & 17600 \\\\\n60613 & 128 & R & 84.272 & 700 & $>24000$ \\\\\n60613 & 128 & N & 98.353 & 300 & 17600 \\\\\n60613 & 128 & Z & 0.000 & 200 & $>24000$ \\\\\n70717 & 32 & S & 88.842 & 2400 & $>24000$ \\\\\n70717 & 32 & R & 54.570 & 11100 & $>24000$ \\\\\n70717 & 32 & N & 50.797 & 4500 & $>24000$ \\\\\n70717 & 32 & Z & 0.744 & 1400 & $>24000$ \\\\\n70717 & 128 & S & 97.768 & 200 & 19200 \\\\\n70717 & 128 & R & 80.659 & 700 & $>24000$ \\\\\n70717 & 128 & N & 97.768 & 200 & 19200 \\\\\n70717 & 128 & Z & 0.000 & 200 & $>24000$ \\\\\n80821 & 32 & S & 73.433 & 3000 & $>24000$ \\\\\n80821 & 32 & R & 65.037 & 9600 & $>24000$ \\\\\n80821 & 32 & N & 70.829 & 3400 & $>24000$ \\\\\n80821 & 32 & Z & 0.797 & 1300 & $>24000$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[derived/observations.json#/rows]]\n\\end{table}\n\nThe full machine-readable record \\path{derived/observations.json} also lists initial and final global norms, parameter counts, initial/final state hashes, updates, and recorded condition durations. It is deterministically extracted from the immutable evidence registry rather than a new evaluation. At width 128, comparison of initial and final hashes and the non-wall-time checkpoint metrics confirms the identity of the studied and norm-matched observations. Their runtime measurements need not coincide, so this identity concerns model states and scientific metrics, not elapsed time.\n# Global Memory Summary\n\nUse as qualitative context only; do not use as quantitative grounding source.\n\n## Recent Entries\n\n- 2026-11-12 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Selected Separating Width Effects from the Post-Fit Decay Clock; idea_bundle_written and execution_pending. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=No experimental evidence or validated refinement is available; an untested design is not a scientific conclusion.\n  - evidence: The validated manifest reports combined_score=0.7779622741764081 and correct=true; histories and prior memory have no experimental results. The referenced baseline source was unavailable, which does not establish a protocol defect. Considered a descriptive width-only sweep and capacity-confounded frozen-feature controls; selected a directly runnable paired width-by-decay intervention with censoring-aware analysis. No training, evaluator execution, computational probes, package installation, or web access occurred. Canonical local memory target is ideas/01_separating_width_effects_from_the_post_fit_decay_clock/memory.md; no alternate cycle-local target was created.\n- 2026-11-12 | step=verify | scope=idea\n  - summary: Manually checked matching rubric content and one canonical local memory target; clarified immediate events, exact event-rate intervals, and informative-censoring limitations. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=Prospective safeguards do not constitute experimental findings.\n  - evidence: Both bundles share four rubric objects; the three implementation verifications contain five, six, and five executable literal snippets. Only ideas/01_separating_width_effects_from_the_post_fit_decay_clock/memory.md was used for local memory. No computational validation or experimental execution occurred.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\ncentral_question: \"How does hidden width relate to the separation between training-set fitting and held-out generalization in a fixed modular-addition network under finite training horizons and matched regularization controls?\"\nnotes_for_writer: [\"This stage produces a strategy only. Do not draft paper.tex, claims_grounding.json, run new training, generate figures, retrieve literature, or run final-paper validation here.\", \"VENUE.md selects ICLR 2026, anonymous conference paper, 8-9 body pages excluding references. The inspected supplied paper.sty is single-column with 5.5in textwidth and 9in textheight. full_width therefore means the full single column, not a two-column figure*. There is no distinct narrow conference column to target. Retain selected formatting; the age comments in the supplied style do not independently certify current official-template provenance, which must be checked before final submission without changing geometry to fit content.\", \"Target about 8.8 body pages: title/abstract/introduction and early overview 1.5; related work 0.75; model, interventions, event estimands, and design 2.0; results including coverage table and two quantitative figures 2.8; discussion, uncertainty, identification limits, and limitations 1.45; conclusion 0.3. These allocations include figures and captions, not extra pages. If the final body is 8 pages, its last page must be at least 75% full; never satisfy the budget with repeated caveats or filler.\", \"Use thesis-first headings: Introduction; Related Work; Width Comparisons and Event Definitions; Experimental Design and Evidence Coverage; Width-Dependent Fitting and Held-Out Accuracy; Delayed Events and Matched Controls; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion. Spend available space explaining initialization coupling, comparator confounding, delayed-event definitions, descriptive seed uncertainty, and the missing final contrast rather than search chronology.\", \"A falsifiable explanation to discuss as a hypothesis, not a discovery: width can change fitting speed separately from the process governing held-out generalization, and initial norm or decay may condition that separation. Earlier fitting without prompt generalization is consistent with that separation; norm collapse, a decay-mediated mechanism, and a universal monotone width effect remain unestablished. These are retrospective tests/interpretations, not preregistered predictions.\", \"Private-only qualification mapping: final.qualification.status=completed, final.qualification.outcome=not_qualified, final.qualification.correct=false. Visible paper wording must explicitly state that final evaluation completed but did not qualify. Repeat this limitation in abstract, results, discussion, and evidence audit; preserve it during review and revision. Exact failing diagnostics are unavailable. Neither retained metrics nor a publication-ready format upgrades scientific qualification.\", \"Private-only source map: final.evidence.episodes binds /final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes. Selected short-horizon raw evidence is at /best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes, matching /all_programs/1/evaluation_evidence/episodes. result.top_program_01 and result.best_correct_score are selection summary sources, not raw scientific metric substitutes. Candidate-reported diagnostics are labeled unverified; do not use their provenance as a validity certification.\", \"Private-only campaign guardrail: no downstream_study or study.* sources exist. Do not infer scored/unscored attempt counts or score improvements, or import an unavailable campaign baseline. Compare the actual fixed-reference arms instead. The locally inspected controller configuration and final record configuration agree; do not imply a broader independent source-freeze audit than the supplied evidence supports.\", \"Final evidence: three retained width32 records, two retained width128 records, no retained width256 records, out of nine planned width-seed cells. The two width128 delayed events are at seeds60613 and70717; the missing width128 cell is seed80821. Separate absent records from recorded right-censored trajectories in text, plots, denominators, and all later revisions.\", \"Private-only aggregate semantics: final.metric.grokking_incidence=0.2222222222222222 is a nine-cell design-weighted aggregate; it is not an unbiased population event probability. final.metric.mean_final_accuracy=0.48813319164009916 includes failure weighting, not the mean of five recorded endpoint accuracies. final.metric.restricted_mean_delay=0.9458333333333333 is a normalized capped functional, not an observed mean time-to-generalization. Keep these aggregates out of headline figures; if retained in an audit table, define the weighting and do not label absent values measured zero.\", \"The reference differs from the studied condition in learning rate and initialization. The zero-decay intervention has finite-horizon scope. Initial-global-norm matching does not match capacity, function, representations, or effective regularization; at width128 it is identical to the studied condition. No circuit, lazy-to-rich transition, random-label control, frozen-feature control, width-by-decay grid, or positive-decay clock collapse was measured.\", \"Report every seed observation and paired contrasts where data exist, with no example/checkpoint pseudoreplication. If reproducible seed-level uncertainty is computed downstream, label it exploratory and conditional on retained records; tiny-sample bootstrap intervals do not prove significance, equivalence, or lack of an effect. Do not silently use 128-minus32 as if it were the planned 256-minus32 contrast.\", \"Include a compact main-text 3-by-3 final availability table, with separate event/censored/missing states and a clear seed key. Essential missingness and failed qualification cannot be relegated to Supplementary Information. Keep separate reader-facing meanings for training fit (0.99), held-out onset (0.95), and delayed event (onset separation at least1000 updates).\", \"Use one consistent four-arm palette across quantitative figures, method names rather than source-directory names, and at least8.5pt text at final size. Numerical displays must be generated from actual recorded data by retained scripts. Export quantitative PDF and 300-DPI PNG, include PDF whenever present, and regenerate raster-only figures at least2400px full-width or1200px column-width; never upscale.\", \"Use exactly one required generate-concept-figure overview, early on page1 or2. Downstream orchestration owns its rendering and any marker. Do not generate it in this stage or silently replace its workflow; record an actual failure before choosing a fallback. No illustrative learning curves or fabricated numeric cartoons.\", \"Literature scope is restricted to authorized starter originals. references.bib and references_provenance.json contain retrieval metadata, not proof that a paper was read; check identities and consult only allowed original works before citation. Use Power for the phenomenon, Loshchilov/Hutter for AdamW, Liu for norm hypotheses, and permitted Kumar/Varma/Nanda/Davies/Nakkiran originals only where needed as distinct theoretical or architectural comparators. Do not inspect citation neighborhoods, recommendations, or unidentified related-work hits, and do not infer the forbidden paper identity.\", \"After references insert a page break and Supplementary Information with substantive grounded subsections; include a separate LLM Usage section there. Preserve anonymization and omit author identities, acknowledgements, funding, and self-identifying links. Route authoritative later appendix-ranked analyses to the supplement without retroactively editing this strategy or hiding essential evidence.\"]\nstaged_input_impact: [\"No previous_paper, aggregated, extra_tasks, or edit-context inputs are present; there is no manuscript to preserve, broaden, or recenter.\", \"Direct inspection of the local selected controller and evaluator establishes one fixed embedding-sum GELU architecture with common hyperparameters, not a new architecture or adaptive training method.\", \"The final_evaluation block is independently decisive: completed but not_qualified, with five retained width-seed records and four missing records. It changes the framing from an optimization-success narrative to a conditional, incomplete-evidence study.\", \"downstream_study and study.* grounding sources are absent. Do not invent campaign-level selection margins, scored/unscored counts, or a campaign baseline comparison. Use the configuration explicitly present in final records and the measured fixed-reference arms.\"]\nedit_framing_decision: \"No edit mode, prior manuscript, staged replacement implementation, or resolved edit-context file is present. The status value denotes rejection of an inherited untested mechanism story, not rejection of an existing paper. Choose a fresh conditional width-and-horizon framing from current records; do not inherit a decay-clock thesis from memory.\"\n1: The final evaluation completed, did not qualify, and has a false qualification decision; the supplied diagnostics do not identify the cause.\n2: Five of nine planned final width-seed records are retained, with coverage 3/2/0 at widths 32/128/256; absent records are not measured non-events or accuracy losses.\n3: The two retained width-128 studied final records show delayed events; all three width-32 held-out onsets are right-censored at 24000 updates.\n4: Development fitting onsets in seed order 101/211 are 2200/2800 at width32, 300/300 at width128, and 200/200 at width256.\n5: Development widest-minus-narrowest paired fit differences are -2000 and -2600 updates; fitting is earlier but endpoint accuracy is nonmonotone in width in both retained seeds.\n6: Development studied endpoint means plus sample SD are 74.68+/-4.47, 29.33+/-3.68, and 39.35+/-3.49 percent at widths32/128/256, over two seeds each.\n7: The task is modulo97 addition, learned by two summed embeddings, GELU, and linear output, with widths32/128/256 and 9441/37473/74849 parameters.\n8: Development/final pools have4704/4705 disjoint pairs with per-seed train/evaluation sizes2822/1882 and2823/1882, respectively; measured results cover one modular operation.\n9: The studied learning-rate/decay/initialization configuration is(0.003,1,0.5), common to all retained widths and seeds; reference(0.001,1,1) differs in two settings.\n10: Zero decay changes only the studied decay to0; norm matching rescales to the same-seed width128 global norm, not to equal capacity or function.\n11: Width128 norm-matched scientific curves and initial/final hashes duplicate the studied records; they are not additional independent delayed events.\n12: Development uses seeds101/211 at6000 updates; final uses60613/70717/80821 at24000, with checkpoints every100 updates; pools and seeds differ along with duration.\n13: Training-fit/held-out onsets require thresholds0.99/0.95 sustained for three consecutive checkpoints, timestamped at the first; a delayed event requires at least1000 updates between onsets.\n14: Final width128 seeds60613/70717 fit at300/200 and generalize at17600/19200; observed delays are17300/19000 updates; endpoints98.353/97.768 percent.\n15: Final width32 seeds60613/70717/80821 fit at2600/2400/3000, have censored held-out onset at24000, and endpoints80.925/88.842/73.433 percent.\n16: The retained seed-paired final width128-minus32 endpoint differences are17.428 and8.927 percentage points, not the planned256-minus32 contrast.\n17: Conditional on retained final records, event counts2/2 and0/3 have exact95-percent binomial intervals[0.158,1] and[0,0.708]; these exploratory intervals neither resolve missingness nor establish population or mechanistic validity.\n18: Retained final no-decay width32 endpoints0.478--0.797 percent and width128 endpoints0 percent coexist with fit onsets1300--1600 and200, respectively; this is a finite-horizon configuration contrast only.\n19: Development norm-matched width32 endpoints70.510/56.164 percent exceed width128 endpoints31.934/26.727 in both seeds; scalar norm matching does not remove that ordering.\n20: Retained final reference endpoints54.570--65.037 percent atwidth32 and80.659--84.272 atwidth128 show no sustained held-out threshold crossing; these are multi-setting reference comparisons.\n21: Final event fraction0.222222 is design-weighted over nine cells, mean endpoint functional0.488133 includes missing-cell zero weighting, and capped delay0.945833 includes missing-cell cap weighting; these are not population event rates, observed endpoint means, or uncensored mean event times.\n22: The descriptive mean of the five observed final studied endpoints is0.878640, distinct from the failure-weighted registry functional.\n23: Related development settings are(0.003,1,1),(0.003,1,0.5),(0.006,0.5,0.5); the faster setting has an unavailable width256 seed211 record, not a measured zero or known failure cause.\n24: The evaluated source uses CPU float32, full-batch AdamW, deterministic algorithms, at most two CPU threads and one inter-op thread; requirements pin numpy2.1.3 and torch2.5.1+cpu.\n25: The readable final evaluator digest matches the registry evaluator digest; hash agreement is provenance, while code contents support architecture and optimizer details.\n26: Recorded selected-development seed evaluation durations are981/754 seconds, and final seed totals3199/2794/3488 seconds after rounding; incomplete final coverage prevents extrapolating complete-grid runtime.\n27: All five retained final studied endpoints have training accuracy1.0, with different residual train cross-entropies; initial/final global norms increase in every record, including delayed-event records. Table norms/losses are individual measured endpoints, not a norm-trajectory mediation test.\n28: Power et al. document delayed generalization on small algorithmic datasets.\n29: Nanda et al. reverse-engineer a Fourier-based modular-addition computation in small Transformers; our architecture and measurements do not establish the same mechanism.\n30: Liu et al. relate grokking to loss landscapes as a function of weight norm.\n31: Varma et al. propose differing learning speeds and norm efficiencies of memorizing and generalizing circuits.\n32: Kumar et al. study lazy-to-rich dynamics, including polynomial regression exhibiting grokking without regularization.\n33: Davies et al. connect grokking and double descent through pattern-learning speeds and capacity variation.\n34: Nakkiran et al. document nonmonotone generalization as model size or duration varies; the present narrow sweep does not establish double descent.\n35: AdamW decouples weight decay from adaptive loss-gradient updates; nominal shrinkage is not a measured complete dynamical clock.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
